nday morning in 2016 he summed up the  city it's the greatest outdoor museum in  the world bill helm Reich was 74 there's  no place in the world like Detroit it's  the most authentic city in America and  I'll challenge anybody on that Marlo  stottlemyre was a tireless cheerleader  for the Motor City a business consultant  who championed young black professionals  he called his Young Lions Marlo a friend  said was the type of man who could see  you before you saw yourself he leaves  his wife Valencia who we met in 9th  grade biology and their two kids  Marlo stottlemyre was 43  Sunday rudder was a hero to her six kids  in Everett Washington after her husband  died in 2012 Sunday went to college kept  a job and still ferried her children to  sporting events and on special trips  diagnosed with breast cancer last year  she battled through but couldn't beat  the coronavirus she was a light her  oldest daughter Alexis says Sunday  rudder was 42 Jazmin Dixon known for her  huge smile worked at the Red Cross in  st. Louis but wanted to open a baking  business in February when some family  members couldn't make her birthday party  she drove around delivering cake to them  last month Jazmin Dixon became the first  person in st. Louis to die of  coronavirus she was 31 so many  remarkable folks we've already lost to  this virus we will continue to pay  tribute to them here and on social media  Gail that's why Anthony you know you  hear the numbers and you hear the  statistics and behind every single  statistic is a person who was loved who  was giving love and receiving love it  just shows you once again this  coronavirus does not discriminate it is  an equal opportunity heartbreaker that  was tough but I'm so glad you did it  it's so important to put a human face on  what is happening to all of us yeah I  totally agree and of course I also think  of our colleague Maria Mercator and all  of this the mind reels at the idea of  all these different people born in  different years